The selectivity comes from **site isolation (a “pseudo-dilution” effect)**, not from a different chemical reactivity of the active sulfur group.

The dichlorosulfoxide-type resin contains polymer-bound electrophilic sulfur sites, essentially chlorosulfonium/dichlorosulfoxide groups, that oxidize an alcohol stoichiometrically. The alcohol oxygen attacks the electrophilic sulfur to form a polymer-bound alkoxysulfonium chloride intermediate; base then promotes elimination/collapse to the carbonyl compound while the sulfur site is reduced to a sulfide. Thus **one active site oxidizes one hydroxyl group**:

\[
\text{Resin-S(Cl)}_2 + \text{ROH}
\rightarrow \text{Resin-S}^+(\text{OR})\text{Cl}
\rightarrow \text{R=O} + \text{Resin-S}
\]

For hexanediol:

\[
\text{HO(CH}_2\text{)}_6\text{OH}
\rightarrow \text{Resin-bound monoalkoxysulfonium intermediate}
\rightarrow \text{HO(CH}_2\text{)}_5\text{CHO}
\]

Over-oxidation would require the remaining terminal –OH in 6-hydroxyhexanal to encounter a second active oxidizing site and be converted further, e.g. to the dialdehyde.

With a **low-functionality resin**, the active oxidizing groups are present at low concentration and are thus widely separated along/within the polymer matrix. A single hexanediol molecule cannot easily bind simultaneously to two neighboring active sites, and after one end has been oxidized and released as 6-hydroxyhexanal, the chance that its remaining –OH immediately finds another active site is low. The second oxidation therefore becomes a separate, statistically unfavorable encounter. This is the key **site-isolation/pseudo-dilution** mechanism.

In contrast, a high-functionality resin has a high local concentration of active sites. Adjacent or clustered sites can cooperate: one end of the diol may be activated while the other end is close enough to another active site to be oxidized before the mono-oxidized product diffuses away. This favors double oxidation/over-oxidation.

Practically, low functionality suppresses over-oxidation but may slow conversion. To maximize conversion while preserving selectivity, one uses a low-site-density resin with sufficient total equivalents of active sites, longer reaction time, or multiple/recycled resin batches. The example in the scheme reflects this trade-off: 51% 6-hydroxyhexanal and 5% recovered hexanediol, with the low-loading resin favoring unilateral oxidation.

Choose an **ultraviolet (UV) light source**, preferably a narrow-band near-UV source with output at **about 342 nm or shorter** (i.e., photon energy at least about **3.63 eV**). This is because the required bond dissociation energy is approximately **350 kJ/mol**, and photochemical bond breaking requires the energy of a single photon to be at least this value.

Calculation:

\[
E_{\text{photon}}=\frac{350\ \text{kJ/mol}}{N_A}
=\frac{350{,}000\ \text{J/mol}}{6.022\times10^{23}\ \text{mol}^{-1}}
\approx 5.81\times10^{-19}\ \text{J}
\]

Using the figure’s conversion \(1\ \text{eV}=96.49\ \text{kJ/mol}\):

\[
E_{\text{photon}}=\frac{350}{96.49}\approx 3.63\ \text{eV}
\]

The corresponding threshold wavelength is:

\[
\lambda=\frac{hc}{E}
\approx \frac{1240\ \text{eV}\cdot\text{nm}}{3.63\ \text{eV}}
\approx 342\ \text{nm}
\]

So photons with **wavelength \(\lambda \lesssim 342\ \text{nm}\)** have sufficient per-photon energy to break a ~350 kJ/mol bond. This wavelength lies in the **ultraviolet region** (near-UV/UVA region, close to the UV–visible boundary).

Why not visible or infrared? Visible light has photons of roughly 1.8–3.1 eV, and infrared photons have even lower energy. Thus typical visible or IR photons have **less than 3.63 eV** per photon and cannot directly supply enough energy to break the 350 kJ/mol bond. For example, a 365 nm UV lamp gives about:

\[
E=\frac{1240}{365}\approx 3.40\ \text{eV}
\]

which corresponds to only:

\[
3.40\times96.49\approx328\ \text{kJ/mol}
\]

so 365 nm is slightly below the required threshold for a 350 kJ/mol bond.

Why not much higher-energy light? Very short-wavelength UV, X-rays, or gamma rays have photons far above the required energy. Excess photon energy is not selective: it can ionize molecules, cleave many different bonds indiscriminately, degrade the polymer backbone, create unwanted radicals or side reactions, and cause discoloration. Deep UV also tends to be absorbed very strongly near the surface, giving poor penetration and greater safety/processing concerns.

Therefore, the engineer should select a **UV source with photons of energy \(\geq 3.63\) eV, corresponding to \(\lambda \leq 342\) nm**, ideally a narrow-band near-UV source close to this threshold to achieve selective crosslinking while avoiding excessive bond scission and material damage.
